The question is: "An exception thrown inside a finally block has no effect on the result of the try block. True or false?"

In PHP, if an exception is thrown in a `finally` block, it will actually replace/suppress any exception that was being thrown from the `try` block. The exception in the `finally` block takes precedence and will be the one that propagates upward.

For example:
```php
try {
    throw new Exception("try exception");
} finally {
    throw new Exception("finally exception");
}
```
In this case, the "finally exception" is what gets thrown, not the "try exception".

So the statement "An exception thrown inside a finally block has no effect on the result of the try block" is FALSE. It does have an effect — it overrides/replaces the exception from the try block.

The answer is "no" (false).

